Findings: full finding list from strict mode.

1. docker-build-without-layer-cache at .github/workflows/docker.yml:13:15
   Message: Job "docker" uses docker/build-push-action without cache-from and cache-to configuration.
   Why: Without layer caching, every CI run rebuilds all Docker layers from scratch, adding minutes per build. docker/build-push-action supports cache-from and cache-to natively; the simplest setup uses the GitHub Actions cache backend.
   Suggested action: Add cache-from and cache-to to the docker/build-push-action step. For example: `cache-from: type=gha` and `cache-to: type=gha,mode=max`. If this build also runs on pull requests, note GHA cache entries written there are readable only within that same PR and mode=max exports every stage: prefer `mode=min` on PR runs or keep the export for branch builds only, and keep mode=max for branch builds.
   Measurement hint: Compare Docker build wall-clock time before and after adding layer caching, including any export upload time on PR runs and eviction of branch-scoped entries. A multi-minute reduction is common for images with several layers.
   Rule docs: https://ci-perf-lint.veritycost.com/rules/docker-build-without-layer-cache
2. prefer-native-arm-runner-over-qemu at .github/workflows/docker.yml:11:15
   Message: Job "docker" uses QEMU for ARM Docker builds targeting linux/arm64.
   Why: This job builds ARM Docker images through QEMU emulation for linux/arm64, which is often slower and less reliable than using a native arm64 runner for the same target.
   Suggested action: If this job primarily targets linux/arm64, consider a native arm64 runner instead of QEMU emulation.
   Measurement hint: Compare wall-clock build time, cache reuse, and failure rate between the current QEMU path and a native arm64 or split-platform build path.
   Rule docs: https://ci-perf-lint.veritycost.com/rules/prefer-native-arm-runner-over-qemu
3. missing-concurrency at .github/workflows/docker.yml:4:3
   Message: The workflow has no workflow-level or job-level concurrency setting.
   Why: Superseded read-only checks can waste runner time, but publication and deployment runs may need to finish and retain every queued release.
   Suggested action: Cancel superseded read-only PR/branch checks only, preferably at job level. Do not cancel release/publish/deploy work. For serialized publication consider cancel-in-progress: false with queue: max (up to 100 pending); this is not a lossless release queue or a dispatch-order guarantee.
   Measurement hint: For read-only checks, confirm superseded runs stop without canceling unrelated checks. For publication, verify in-progress completion, queued-release retention and required ordering under bursts.
   Rule docs: https://ci-perf-lint.veritycost.com/rules/missing-concurrency